  "name": "Example: Solving Inequalities Requiring Multiplication by a Positive Factor",
  "problem": "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{5x^4 +2} \\lt \\dfrac{5-2x}{5x^4+2}.\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "We know that the denominator $5x^4+ 2$ is positive for all values of $x.$ So, we can multiply both sides of the inequality by $5x^4+2$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5{x}^{4}+2} & <\\frac{5-2x}{5{x}^{4}+2} \\\\ (5{x}^{4}+2)\\cdot \\frac{2}{5{x}^{4}+2} & <(5{x}^{4}+2)\\cdot \\frac{5-2x}{5{x}^{4}+2} \\\\ 2 & <5-2x \\\\ 2x & <3 \\\\ x & <\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
